Germany's Defense Minister was a la  fonda line and her British counterpart  Gavin Williamson have kicked off the  Munich Security Conference it's a three  days of high-stakes debate and  closed-door meetings the event is  bringing together an unprecedented  number of international decision-makers  including the largest US delegation ever  a multitude of international  organizations are also represented  including the International Monetary  Fund the World Bank and the UN well the  German defence ministers we said kick  things off emphasizing the importance of  partners cooperation our correspondence  with us here at mocs Koscheck is with us  here in studio and assignment Jung is  over in Munich let's listen first to  what the defense minister German defense  minister had to say about the importance  of NATO and cooperation one's a  powerpuff match our partnership makes it  stronger and it increases our  sovereignty because in our partnership  interests are consistent with our values  well to some the world is a zero-sum  game we see the opportunity for growing  together and reminds a prospering  together in those partnerships this is  why ladies and gentlemen NATO remains  our first choice when it comes to our  security  [Applause]  alright Simon let's come to you first we  heard was off on the line they're saying  NATO remains our first choice and we  heard Gavin Williamson also say NATO is  the bedrock of our security again remind  us why is it so important to reiterate  that commitment it would seem obvious  that NATO is an important part of  security well I think because some have  in recent years begun to question  whether NATO does have the answers of  course in particular President Trump in  Washington has raised doubts he  suggested that NATO is no longer fit for  purpose that it doesn't answer the  challenges he's particularly concerned  of course about what he sees as a  failure by the European allies to pay  their fair share of defence spending he  wants to hold them to their commitment  to increase that to 2% of GDP Germany  for instance is one of many countries  that haven't reached that target but the  German defence minister staying here  again today you know that Germany's on  its way it's getting there  Europe needs to do more she said the  Americans are right to criticize us in  that way but she wanted to make the case  for a much wider vision of what NATO is  about as a defender of values of human  rights of human dignity so she was  really widening the vision of what a  defence community needs to do it doesn't  just need to be about cash capabilities  it needs to be about values and  democracy as well she said max I want to  ask you about what Simon just mentioned  you know we heard was left on the line  talk about the return of competition  between great powers and distinguished  NATO from other alliances that exist in  the world and and talked as Simon said  about values and interests but also  about fairness what was she getting at  from your perspective  well she was obviously contrasting the  NATO treaty  to other alliance that they're currently  being left by China and Russia for  example the Belton rote initiative  that's currently built by China and the  Eurasian Union that Russia  building up and what she was doing she  was contrasting NATO against these two  alliances in the sense that these are  not that NATO is not alliance it's built  on dominance she was saying we're in the  lines of equal partners of equal members  or these alliances he led by Russia and  China and led by a dominant power now  Gavin Williamson has also actually been  speaking about NATO as we said and also  talking about the importance of NATO  despite Britain leaving the EU as we  know in just six weeks time let's listen  to his comments but as we continue to  face threats in an increasingly  dangerous world we know that NATO is the  best guardian for all of our security so  for the sake of our values and friends  we will continue to lead a NATO will  continue to build our alliances with  close friends like Germany will continue  to deliver European security will  continue to step out into the world  protecting our friends defending our  interests standing up for our values  all right so Simon very clear that the  German and British defense ministers are  on message sharing the very same message  here that NATO is key but we have again  heard the call for more joint spending  and acting from EU states there's a very  large in fact the largest US delegation  ever at the Munich Security Conference  there at this weekend we know that these  bilateral talks that happen at the  conference it happened behind closed  doors are also really important what we  see then talks precisely on this issue  with the US where there's been so much  friction  well I think that certainly will happen  it will happen it remains to be seen but  you always get a major a Russian  delegation here in Munich and as you say  the largest ever congressional  delegation from the US this year and  indeed vice president Mike Pence will be  here as well so he'll have an  opportunity to meet his Russian  counterpart Sergei Lavrov they've got a  lot to talk about both countries have  said they're pulling out of the INF  nuclear forces treaty that really is one  of the cornerstones of the sort of  post-war post Cold War security order in  Europe so that is an absolutely massive  focus here what Gavin Williamson was  saying there was again this idea of a  values driven NATO but he had a slightly  different emphasis as well one of his  key messages of course is that Britain  will continue to play a role in European  security even after it leaves the  European Union a lot of people have been  concerned that that might not happen but  he said no will remain a stable partner  we'll do our best and incidentally on  European defence cooperation he said we  welcome that we would like to see new  countries and going forward of course  he's talking about joining together  Germany and France you know they've got  this scheme of a European army for the  future well you know he's saying we like  that a kind of idea because it means  there are more capabilities and NATO can  do more max I want to ask you about this  question of European defence cooperation  this is a refrain we've heard over and  over from the German defense minister  but also from a German leaders many  German leaders that Europe needs to take  its own defence and security into its  own hands we heard this at last year's  Mena security conference as well has  this really materialized have we seen  real progress on that front not per se  progress that we see more of a debate or  distinguishing understanding of what's  different parts of Europe want in that  so what's quite interesting to see in  her speech or sofa laFonda line was  actually explaining that both the United  Kingdom and Germany are taking part in  the NATO enhanced forward presence NATO  mission there was introduced  used after the Ukraine crisis to kind of  put more presence forward in the Baltic  countries and in Poland and both the  United Kingdom and Germany are doing air  patrolling there and we're cellphone  aligned was saying in her speech it's  both the United Kingdom - Jets and the  German euro fighter jets and that  already explains one of the big problems  we're currently having in Europe there's  too much difference in the different  military to capabilities they're not  streamlined that were using different  Jets equipment and that's what's the big  kind of current challenge for European  military alliances to kind of have a  streamlined joint force because so many  different countries are using different  equipment okay so that's one of the  hiccups for European defence of the  issues that we heard assignment just  mentioned because because this is a big  picture conference talking about the  various security challenges in the world  right now what do you think are the most  important that will be discussed this  weekend especially for Germany the host  well what we have heard already from a  German defense that was the fatherland  and what we also would like to hear  probably from the other German speakers  were coming in the upcoming days is how  they're going to keep pushing the  message of promoting multilateralism the  German Chancellor Angela Merkel has said  she wants to kind of talk about that on  their unique he could security  conference in the upcoming days and  we've already heard or sort of  underlined giving a sort of the first  pack of that promoting alliances  promoting multilateralism and giving a  clear statement in the theme of the  Munich Security Conference of  fragmentation in the world that this is  an option how to defend it yes saving  the liberal world order that is very  much a defining this year's conference  okay our correspondents max Coe secure  in studio and Simon Young down on the  floor there in Munich thank you both  we'll talk to you bit later

